workedExample:
eyes -> voice -> copy -> paste. v1 covers the AUTHORITY/approval spine of this flow first-class; the focus/target/transfer machinery is Phase 2. Here is the settled end-to-end shape (C#-shaped), marked by phase.

[PHASE 2 — focus + target] The gaze source produces an Attention candidate: gazeSource.Tell(new UpdateAttention(actorId, attentionTargetRef, confidence)). The FocusActor (single writer) sets FocusGraph.Attention only — never Command/Text. No prompt: Observe/Attention for a local human is ImplicitGrant.

[PHASE 2 — voice -> command] The voice source recognizes 'copy this' and emits a CommandRequest{ActorId, CommandId.Copy ('edit.copy'), Target = BestCommandTarget}, where BestCommandTarget = Command ?? Text ?? Pointer ?? Attention ?? Navigation resolves to the gaze Attention target. This is a pure function (FocusGraph.BestCommandTarget), headless-testable.

[PHASE 2 — resolve + offer] The ShellActor forwards to the SurfaceActor, which hit-tests the app scene (ResolveTargetAsync turns the point/attention into an ObjectTarget/RangeTarget). edit.copy for a local human is ImplicitGrant (no prompt), so the surface builds a TransferOffer with Policy/Provenance/Scope and the BrokerActor stores it. For TEXT it also mirrors to ClipboardText through AppHost.Invoke (main thread) with a timeout. For a GPU SURFACE payload this is where the deferred Skyline.Interaction.Gpu bridge hands over a wgpu texture handle — never a CPU round-trip; until that bridge ships, surface copy is explicitly out of scope.

[PHASE 1 — the part v1 makes first-class: an AI/remote tries to paste = the approval] Now an AI actor (Actor{Ai}) issues CommandRequest{CommandId.Paste, Target = a text field}. edit.paste into a text field needs Edit. The policy says Ai -> Ask. THIS is the v1 spine:
  var outcome = await shell.AuthorizeAsync(aiActor, InteractionCapability.Edit, target, prompt: "Planner wants to type into Address", ct);
  // policy.Evaluate(aiActor, Edit) => Ask  => shell calls IApprovalUi.RequestAsync
  // ApprovalsOverlay: create TCS, post snapshot rebuild to the single owner, RequestRedraw
  // render thread: reads volatile snapshot, opens LoadOp.Load pass on Frame.Encoder, draws the modal naming 'Planner (AI)' + 'type into Address' + Allow/Allow once/Deny
  // MAIN thread: human taps Allow -> AppWindowInteractionSource hit-tests -> tcs.TrySetResult(ApprovalDecision.Allow)
  // shell resumes: mints CapabilityGrant{Grantee=aiActor, Capabilities=Edit, RequiresVisibleUi=true}, ConsentRecord{Kind=PromptAccepted}
  // persistent indicator stays lit while the grant lives (the RecordingIndicator analogue)
With the grant, the brokered paste proceeds: TransferPolicy.PermitsPaste(aiActor, dest) must allow AI-readable; the BrokerActor returns the live offer's representation (preferring the in-process offer over the ClipboardText fallback). If the human taps Deny, the TCS resolves Deny, the AI's command is rejected (or forced PreviewOnly at the surface boundary), and nothing mutates.

The whole flow is a sequence of typed messages where every step says WHO. In v1 the WHO + the Edit/keyboard/control approval is the deliverable; in Phase 2 the gaze->Attention->BestCommandTarget->resolve->offer->broker machinery around it becomes first-class.
